# Skills

Give your coding agent deep knowledge of COSS for Svelte components, patterns, and review rules.

<CopyMarkdownButton url="/docs/skills.md" />

Skills give coding agents structured knowledge about a component library: APIs, composition patterns,
styling conventions, migration rules, and common pitfalls. With the right project instructions, an
agent can use COSS primitives without guessing.

For example, you can ask your agent to:

- _"Add a settings dialog with a form and save/cancel buttons."_
- _"Add a select with grouped options and a search filter."_
- _"Migrate this shadcn-svelte menu to COSS for Svelte."_
- _"Build a toast notification for form submission errors."_

## Install

COSS for Svelte does not publish an Agent Skills package yet. Do not install the upstream React skill
and assume its APIs apply to this port.

Agents working in this repository start with `AGENTS.md`. It points to the port specification, the
active plan, and the rules that protect source provenance and component parity. A future public skill
will have its own install command once the Svelte API and registry are stable.

Learn more about the skills ecosystem at [skills.sh](https://skills.sh).

## What's included

The repository instructions cover the full porting workflow.

### Component knowledge

Before changing a component, an agent reads the complete COSS registry source, documentation page,
and every particle that imports it from the permitted `reference/apps/ui` boundary. It then reads the
complete matching Shards source, documentation, tests, examples, and exported types.

### Styling conventions

The instructions cover Tailwind v4 token usage, the semantic color system, Hugeicons sizing,
`data-slot` selectors, `--alpha()` syntax, and the font-variable contract: `--font-sans`,
`--font-mono`, and `--font-heading`.

All interface icons use Hugeicons data through the shared SSR-safe renderer:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { Copy01Icon } from "@hugeicons/core-free-icons";
  import { Button, HugeiconsIcon } from "@coss-sv/ui";
</script>

<Button aria-label="Copy" size="icon" variant="outline">
  <HugeiconsIcon aria-hidden="true" icon={Copy01Icon} strokeWidth={2} />
</Button>
```

### Migration patterns

The rules cover namespace composition, trigger delegation, controlled and uncontrolled bindings,
Select item data, Toggle Group multiple selection, Slider values, and the callback contracts used by
Shards-backed components.

### CLI and registry

The guides document installation through the shadcn-svelte CLI, custom registry URLs, dependency
discovery, and manual install paths.

### Particle examples

Every component review includes the upstream particle catalog. Agents use those real compositions to
port production examples instead of inventing minimal stubs.

## How it works

1. The coding agent reads `AGENTS.md`, the port specification, and the active plan before editing.
2. It reads the complete source evidence for the assigned component or page, then the relevant Svelte
   Edge references.
3. It writes Svelte 5 with `$props()`, snippets, callback props, typed contexts, native attributes,
   and hydration-stable IDs.
4. It uses the matching COSS particles and the live reference instead of relying on memory.
5. Parity, accessibility, and Svelte-quality reviewers repeat the source inspection before approving
   the exact commit.

## Supported agents

These instructions are plain repository files and can be read by any agent that supports project
instructions, including Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Cline, Windsurf, and GitHub Copilot. An agent is
not considered compatible until it performs the same source, test, browser, and review workflow.
